    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Syndicalists held that unions, not political parties, should wage the class struggle by direct action and the general strike, and that union federations would manage production and replace the state after a revolution.</li><li>Syndicalist organisations such as the CGT in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the CNT in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, the USI in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the FAUD in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the FORA in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> reached their height between about 1910 and the early 1920s, and in Spain from 1936 put much of the economy under workers' control until the Nationalist victory.</li><li>Scholars disagree on the word itself: some apply it to every revolutionary, direct-action union movement, including the IWW, while others, such as Larry Peterson, use five criteria that exclude the IWW and the Canadian One Big Union.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-joseph-proudhon">Pierre-Joseph Proudhon</a>, a root of syndicalist thought, coined the word capitalist, and Émile Pouget said that the idea of the general strike came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, fertilised by the blood of the anarchists hanged in Chicago.</p></aside>
    </section>

