    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1895</span><span class="rf-text">The French General Confederation of Labour (CGT) is formed; it advocates the general strike from the start</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1905</span><span class="rf-text">The Industrial Workers of the World is founded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1906</span><span class="rf-text">The CGT adopts the Charter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a>, reaffirming its independence from party politics</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1912</span><span class="rf-text">The Italian syndicalist confederation USI is formed by veterans of the farm and factory strikes</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1913</span><span class="rf-text">The First International Syndicalist Congress meets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> but does not found an international</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text">The Free Workers' Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> (FAUD) is formed in December</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1922</span><span class="rf-text">The International Workers' Association is formed as an international syndicalist federation</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1936</span><span class="rf-text">The Spanish Revolution puts much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s economy under workers' control, with the CNT as the main union organisation of the Republican side</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-joseph-proudhon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-joseph-proudhon.jpg" alt="Pierre-Joseph Proudhon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-joseph-proudhon">Pierre-Joseph Proudhon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Pierre_Joseph_Proudhon_1865.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gustave Courbet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Syndicalism is a revolutionary socialist current within the labour movement. The word comes from the French syndicat, a trade union, and the concept of syndicalisme révolutionnaire emerged in French socialist journals in 1903, when the General Confederation of Labour (CGT) came to use it for its own brand of unionism. Its direct roots lay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-joseph-proudhon">Pierre-Joseph Proudhon</a>'s mutualism, popular in the anti-authoritarian wing of the International Workingmen's Association formed in 1864. Its leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-bakunin">Mikhail Bakunin</a> came to believe that worker organisations might need to use force; he and his followers advocated the general strike, rejected electoral politics and anticipated workers' organisations replacing rule by the state. Lucien van der Walt holds that the Spanish Regional Federation of that International, formed in 1870, was already syndicalist.</p><p>The Haymarket Affair in Chicago in 1886 led anarchists and labour organisers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to rethink the revolutionary meaning of the general strike. In the 1890s French anarchists, concluding that individual action such as assassination had failed, turned to the labour movement and gained influence in the bourses du travail, which formed a national federation in 1893. The CGT was formed in 1895 and advocated the general strike from the start; the bourses merged into it in 1902, and in 1906 it adopted the Charter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karl-marx.jpg" alt="Karl Marx" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_Marx_001_restored.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Jabez Edwin Mayall, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Syndicalists held that society was divided into two classes, the working class and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a>, whose interests were irreconcilable. Tom Mann declared that the object of the unions was to wage the Class War, aimed not only at higher wages or shorter hours but at the revolutionary overthrow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>. They agreed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> that the state was the executive committee of the ruling class, and held that an economic order could not be overthrown by changes to the political order. Unions therefore declared their political neutrality, on the reasoning that parties united members of different classes while unions united the whole working class.</p><p>Unions played a dual role: they fought for immediate improvements within capitalism and were to carry out the revolution. Syndicalists favoured direct action, among them working to rule, passive resistance, sabotage and strikes, and saw the general strike as the final step towards revolution. They remained vague about the society to follow, saying it could not be foreseen in detail, but generally agreed that production would be managed by workers and the state apparatus replaced by the rule of workers' organisations, with the unions as the embryo of the new society. Many rejected collective bargaining agreements and large strike funds for fear of bureaucracy.</p><p>Policies on gender were mixed. The CNT did not admit women until 1918, and the CGT dismissed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feminism">feminism</a> as a bourgeois movement, though attitudes shifted in some cases.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alceste-de-ambris"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alceste-de-ambris.jpg" alt="Alceste De Ambris" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alceste-de-ambris">Alceste De Ambris</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alceste_de_Ambris_1930.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>French syndicalism and American industrial unionism influenced its rise elsewhere. Activists who had spent time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> founded syndicalist organisations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, Émile Pouget's pamphlets were translated into several languages, and migrants and sailors carried the ideas to other countries. The Industrial Workers of the World (IWW) was formed in 1905 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>; its members insisted theirs was a distinctly American unionism, though most IWW historians, such as Melvyn Dubofsky, regard it as the American form of syndicalism. The Argentine Regional Workers' Federation (FORA) was formed by Italian and Spanish immigrants in 1901.</p><p>Some syndicalists worked within existing unions; others built federations of their own, a strategy known as dual unionism, as with the IWW, the Irish Transport and General Workers' Union, the Italian USI and the Spanish CNT. By 1914 there were syndicalist national confederations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, Spain, Italy and France.</p><p>The First International Syndicalist Congress met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1913 with 38 delegates from 65 organisations but founded no international. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> began, most syndicalists opposed it, but the majority of the CGT and a minority of the USI, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alceste-de-ambris">Alceste De Ambris</a>, supported their countries. The International Workers' Association followed in 1922.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kronstadt-rebellion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kronstadt-rebellion.jpg" alt="Kronstadt rebellion" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kronstadt-rebellion">Kronstadt rebellion</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kronstadt_attack.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Syndicalist movements seldom held power and were mostly under pressure from states and rival socialists. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> the syndicalist Casa del Obrero Mundial allied with the government established after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutionalist-army">Constitutionalist Army</a> occupied the capital in 1914, helping to defeat rural forces such as the Zapatistas, but the army quelled its general strike of July and August 1916 and the Casa was suppressed. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> syndicalists welcomed the soviets and factory committees of 1917, and workers' control was introduced, but it led to economic chaos and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> put the economy under state control and subordinated the committees to trade unions that became organs of the state. Syndicalists, who denounced the regime as state capitalist, were harassed and arrested, and the arrests that followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kronstadt-rebellion">Kronstadt rebellion</a> of 1921 ended the movement.</p><p>The widest application came in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The Spanish Revolution of 1936 brought anarcho-syndicalist and more broadly socialist organisational principles to Catalonia, Aragon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a> and parts of the Levante for two to three years, with the CNT the main union organisation of the Republican side, and much of the economy was put under workers' control before the Nationalists won the civil war and suppressed them.</p><p>Elsewhere repression was severe. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> some 200 workers were killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> alone in 1917 to 1919, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> between 100 and 700 people died in the Tragic Week of 1919.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin.jpg" alt="Vladimir Lenin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lenin_in_1920_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown, presumably official, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Within the labour movement the main attacks came from socialists and communists. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a> posited that revolutionary syndicalism was a direct and inevitable result of opportunism, reformism and parliamentary cretinism. According to many Marxists, syndicalism was a reaction to reformism and could not survive without it. Darlington holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> exposed syndicalism's inherent weaknesses: building organisations meant to be both revolutionary cadres and mass unions, stressing economic struggle over political action, and a commitment to localism that limited effective central leadership. The anarchist Iain McKay argues that syndicalism is a new name for the ideas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-bakunin">Bakunin</a> and the anarchist wing of the First International, and wholly inconsistent with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Engels</a>.</p><p>Historians dispute how far its doctrines were shared by members. Peter Stearns concludes that most French workers did not identify with its long-range goals, while Frederick Ridley holds that it was a genuine product of the French labour movement. Stearns also judged French syndicalism a dismal failure, arguing that its radicalism shocked workers and the government and weakened the movement as a whole. Robert Hoxie, Selig Perlman and Patrick Renshaw attribute the IWW's decline to workers' pragmatism, a view Darlington disputes.</p><p>Georges Sorel was often thought its premier theorist, but he was not part of the movement and his influence was insignificant except in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union.jpg" alt="Solidarity (Polish trade union)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity (Polish trade union)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Strajk_sierpniowy_w_Stoczni_Gda%C5%84skiej_im._Lenina_34.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zenon Mirota, CC BY-SA 3.0 pl</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From the early 1920s syndicalist movements waned in most countries. Authoritarian governments suppressed them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, and the IWW and the Mexican Casa were weakened by repression. Van der Linden and Thorpe attribute the decline of those not suppressed mainly to the integration of the working class into capitalist relations, helped by the welfare state and the widening of the franchise. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-hobsbawm">Eric Hobsbawm</a> saw the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> as the biggest reason. By the end of the 1930s meaningful legal syndicalist organisations existed only in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>. The Nationalist victory in Spain ended it as a mass movement.</p><p>The IWA still exists, with very little influence; Wayne Thorpe called it a flicker of history, the custodian of doctrine. The CNT peaked at over 300,000 members in 1978, then split, and the breakaway CGT had around 100,000 members in 2018. The CNT and the German FAU left the IWA in 2018 to form the International Confederation of Labour. The late 1960s revived interest among activists in Germany, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and in the 1980s the Polish union <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a>, though not strictly syndicalist, revived many of its ideas and practices.</p><p>Darlington notes that the IWW song <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-forever">Solidarity Forever</a> entered the American labour canon, and that the sit-down strike, made famous at Flint, was pioneered by Wobblies in 1906.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Syndicalism is a revolutionary current of the labour movement, taking shape in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the 1890s and 1900s, that looked to industrial unions, direct action and the general strike to overthrow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> and put production under workers' control. It peaked before and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1936, then declined under repression and the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-democracy">social democracy</a>.</em></p>

